language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ bales_of_hay: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ There are a number of bales of hay. Instead of being weighed individually, they were weighed in all possible combinations of two. The weights of these combinations were written down and arranged in numerical order, without keeping track of which weight matched which pair of bales. Find the weight of each bale from the list of pair weights.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `bales_of_hay.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/bales_of_hay.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `bales_of_hay_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py bales_of_hay.json
$   savilerow model.eprime bales_of_hay_1.param -run-solver

$ n: the number of bales
given n : int
$ weights_dim1: the number of pair weights, n(n-1)/2 (derived from the data)
given weights_dim1 : int
$ weights[k]: the k-th pair weight in the list, indices 1-based
given weights : matrix indexed by [int(1..weights_dim1)] of int

$ the total of all pair weights, an upper bound on any bale weight
letting top = sum(weights)
$ PI[k], PJ[k]: the bales of the k-th pair of bales (PI[k] < PJ[k]), pairs in lexicographic order; perm below matches each pair to its weight in the list
letting PI = [ i | i : int(1..n), j : int(1..n), i < j ]
letting PJ = [ j | i : int(1..n), j : int(1..n), i < j ]

$ bales[i]: the weight of bale i, indices 1-based
find bales : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of int(0..top)
$ perm[k]: the position in the list of pair weights that holds the weight of the k-th pair of bales
find perm : matrix indexed by [int(1..weights_dim1)] of int(1..weights_dim1)

such that
$ every pair weight in the list is the weight of exactly one pair of bales
allDiff(perm),
$ the weight of each pair of bales is the sum of the weights of its two bales
forAll k : int(1..weights_dim1) . weights[perm[k]] = bales[PI[k]] + bales[PJ[k]]
